module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.wire_projection_names_a_field_the_envelope_lacks

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data wire_projection_names_a_field_the_envelope_lacks: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "wire_projection_names_a_field_the_envelope_lacks" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a wire projection names a field the envelope does not carry** (a service operation declares an output projected `from \"<path>\"`, the response arrives successfully, the path resolves to nothing, and the operation yields NULL. The call SUCCEEDED -- a real 200 from the real upstream -- so nothing downstream has any reason to doubt the value it was handed.",

    "INVALID STATE: a successful response producing a fabricated absence that is indistinguishable, at the consumer, from a genuine one. This sits BELOW the ladder rather than on it: no typed refusal fires, no diagnostic is emitted, and the consumer's own \"the field was empty\" arm absorbs it.",

    "WHY IT IS WORSE THAN A TRANSPORT FAILURE, which is what makes it its own row rather than an instance of a decode refusal: a transport failure and an undecodable body both REPORT. This reports success. The operation's declared RestOutcome says RestOk, because the outcome describes the HTTP exchange and the projection is a separate step that has no outcome channel at all. So a consumer that dutifully matches every outcome arm -- the discipline this repository enforces everywhere -- still cannot see it.",

    "SPECIMEN, measured live rather than reasoned about (2026-09-10, gunbc#10925 lane). extdeps.cloudflare.account_api_tokens ListPermissionGroupsRaw declares `body: String from \"body\"`. Cloudflare's client/v4 envelope is { result, success, errors, messages } and carries no `body` member. A live GET to /accounts/<id>/tokens/permission_groups, authenticated with a real account token read out of Secret Manager, returned 200 -- the request log shows the exchange -- and the operation produced null. The typed replacement beside it, ListPermissionGroups projecting `groups: List<AccountPermissionGroupRow> from \"result\"`, returned 380-odd rows from the identical call, which is the discriminating pair: same endpoint, same credential, same moment, one projection answering and one fabricating.",

    "THE RAW OPERATION IS DELIBERATELY LEFT STANDING, and that is a decision rather than an oversight. Repairing that one `from` string would close this specimen and leave the CLASS invisible, because nothing in the substrate would have changed: every other operation in the tree remains free to name a field its envelope cannot carry, and the next one fails exactly this quietly. The raw operation keeps its frontier row and stands as the enrolled specimen until the class has a wall.",

    "SCOPE IS THE PROJECTION MECHANISM, NOT THIS OPERATION AND NOT THIS VENDOR. Any `from` path in any declared operation, against any transport, has this property. The population is every projection in the corpus, and the reason none of them is currently checked is that a projection path is validated against nothing -- there is no declared envelope shape for it to be validated AGAINST.",

    "RUNG: found BELOW the ladder -- silent wrongness, which DESIGN section 5 puts outside it entirely rather than at rung 1.",

    "CEILING 4, structurally impossible, and the reason it is the top rung is that the subject is fully decidable and entirely ours. A response type is already declared for each status (`200 => AccountPermissionGroupsListResponse`), so the set of fields an envelope carries is a fact the compiler holds at declaration time. A projection naming a path outside that shape is a decidable mismatch between two things both present in the source.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, stated as the capability rather than as an artifact: projection paths validated against the declared response shape at COMPILE time, so an operation whose `from` names a field the envelope cannot carry refuses at declaration and never runs. The affordable intermediate, which is strictly weaker and must not be mistaken for the trigger, is a projection that refuses at READ time when its path resolves to nothing -- that converts silent wrongness into a typed runtime refusal (rung 1) but leaves the invalid declaration writable.",

    "REVIEW TELL: an operation whose output projections were written from the shape the author WANTED rather than from a captured response. Ask for the envelope. A `from \"body\"` or a `from \"data\"` on an API that wraps its payload -- Cloudflare's `result`, JSON:API's `data`, a GraphQL `data.<field>` -- is the specific shape to distrust, because those are the names a reader assumes when they have not looked.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
